# stm32f767zi.resc -- Renode platform + firmware load for the STM32F767ZI
# (NUCLEO-F767ZI) vendor platform in the holoscan_mcu_lite harness.
#
# WHAT THIS IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT LIKE THE OTHER FOUR .resc FILES
#   The stm32f4, nxp_lpc, ti_tm4c and microchip_sam machines each say in their
#   own header that they deliberately have NO network peripheral, because their
#   smoke images run the codec and reassembly code and never send anything. A
#   plausible-looking Ethernet MAC on those boards would be an invitation to
#   claim coverage that does not exist.
#
#   This one is different, and the difference is the reason the row exists. The
#   STM32F767 has an Ethernet MAC, and Renode MODELS it:
#       ethernet: Network.SynopsysEthernetMAC @ sysbus 0x40028000
#           -> nvic@61
#       (platforms/cpus/stm32f777.repl)
#   and the NUCLEO-F767ZI board description wires a real physical layer to it:
#       phy: Network.EthernetPhysicalLayer @ ethernet 0
#           Id1: 0x0007
#           Id2: 0xC0F1
#       (platforms/boards/nucleo_f767zi.repl)
#
#   So this platform declares that MAC and PHY explicitly rather than deriving
#   them from `mach LoadPlatformDescription`, for two reasons:
#     1. Every other row in this harness spells its memory map out in full, and
#        selfcheck_vendors.sh parses those literals to keep this file, the linker
#        script, board_config.h and the index row in agreement. Deriving the map
#        from Renode's board file would take the map out of the harness's reach.
#     2. Aiming the PHY at a real interface lets the emitted frame leave the
#        machine, which is the entire point: the example asserts on the bytes
#        that come out.
#
# MEMORY MAP (cross-checked by renode/scripts/selfcheck_vendors.sh against
# firmware/platform/gcc_stm32f767zi_hml_mcu.ld, firmware/platform/board_config.h
# and the stm32f767zi row of renode/boards/index -- all four must agree or the
# stm32f767zi_renode_selfcheck CTest test fails)
#   0x08000000  2 MiB   FLASH  code, rodata, .data initialiser image
#   0x20000000  512 KiB SRAM   .data, .bss, stack, newlib heap
#   0x40004800          USART3  the console (UART.STM32F7_USART)
#   0x40028000          ETH     the MAC (Network.SynopsysEthernetMAC)
#
# WHY SRAM IS 512 KiB HERE WHEN Renode's repl DECLARES 0x10000000
#   platforms/cpus/stm32f777.repl gives sram a 256 MiB size, which is a Renode
#   placeholder rather than a property of the part. Modelling all of it would let
#   the image depend on memory no STM32F767ZI has. This platform claims 512 KiB,
#   a subset of what Renode provides, so a memory overrun is a loud failure here
#   instead of a silent success that would not happen on silicon.
#
# STATUS -- READ BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE
#   Claims about this platform belong to the example README and to the robot
#   test's own output, NOT to this file, and only if a run produced them. An
#   emulator is not an MCU, a frame seen on an emulated PHY is not a frame on a
#   wire, and "the transport path executed" is not "the GPU received the data".
#   If the tooling is MISSING, run_vendor_smoke.sh exits 77 and CTest reports
#   Skipped -- a skip is not a pass. If the tooling is PRESENT and the platform
#   does not load, the test FAILS, loudly, and never as an unobserved pass.

:name: stm32f767zi
:description: stm32f767zi vendor platform (STM32F767ZI NUCLEO, Cortex-M7, USART3 @ 0x40004800, Synopsys Ethernet MAC @ 0x40028000 with an EthernetPhysicalLayer, 2 MiB flash, 512 KiB SRAM). The only vendor row with a modelled network peripheral. Emulator only; not silicon.

$elf?=stm32f767zi_hml_mcu_demo.elf

mach create "hml_stm32f767zi"
# THE CONSOLE IS UART.STM32_UART, NOT UART.STM32F7_USART, and that is deliberate.
# The part's own USART model in Renode is UART.STM32F7_USART, but no test in
# Renode's suite covers it, and driving it from this firmware does not work:
# with the documented STM32F7 CR1 (UE|TE = 0x2008 at +0x0C) the register does
# read back as 0x2008, yet the model rejects every character with a
# 'transmitter is not enabled, ignoring' warning and the run can never reach its
# DONE line. Rather than reverse-engineer an untested model's enable condition
# and hard-code a magic value into the image, this platform uses
# UART.STM32_UART, which Renode DOES test:
# tests/peripherals/STM32_UART.robot pins STATUS=0x00, DATA=0x04, BRR=0x08 and
# CR1=0x0C with UE=0x2000 and TE=0x0004, and drives characters through it.
# The console is observability plumbing, not the subject of the test, so it uses
# the model whose behaviour is actually pinned down.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT COST
#   It does not weaken the network claim on this board: the MAC below is still
#   Network.SynopsysEthernetMAC, the part's real MAC model, and that is the
#   peripheral the example drives. It does mean the console register map is that
#   of the STM32_UART model rather than the F7's own USART, which is a deviation
#   from a real STM32F767's USART3 and is recorded here so nobody mistakes this
#   row for a faithful console port.
#
# EDITOR NOTE: this comment sits OUTSIDE the platform string on purpose. Renode's
# description parser rejects a '#' line inside the triple-quoted block, and a
# double-quote anywhere in it terminates the string, so platform comments belong
# here and nowhere else.
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
cpu: CPU.CortexM @ sysbus
    cpuType: "cortex-m7"
    nvic: nvic

nvic: IRQControllers.NVIC @ sysbus 0xE000E000
    -> cpu@0

flash: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x08000000
    size: 0x200000

sram: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x20000000
    size: 0x80000

uart: UART.STM32_UART @ sysbus <0x40004800, +0x100>
    frequency: 200000000
    IRQ -> nvic@39

ethernet: Network.SynopsysEthernetMAC @ sysbus 0x40028000
    -> nvic@61

phy: Network.EthernetPhysicalLayer @ ethernet 0
    Id1: 0x0007
    Id2: 0xC0F1
    AutoNegotiationAdvertisement: 0x00A1
    AutoNegotiationLinkPartnerBasePageAbility: 0x001
"""

sysbus LoadELF $elf
# The Cortex-M takes its initial SP and reset vector from the first two words of
# the region LoadELF placed the image in, so the vector table offset must be the
# image's load address, spelled out literally. selfcheck_vendors.sh asserts this
# line against the FLASH ORIGIN in the linker script.
cpu VectorTableOffset 0x08000000

# The PHY registers itself with the MAC through the `@ ethernet 0` attachment in
# the platform description above, so no explicit registration command is needed
# (renode has no `phy RegisterMac`; an earlier draft of this file invented one).
# The MAC's own address is what the image will put in the Ethernet header when it
# transmits; set it here so the frame the example asserts on has a known source.
sysbus.ethernet MAC "02:00:00:00:00:01"

# No showAnalyzer: under renode-test --console the UART is captured by the robot
# file's Create Terminal Tester, and an interactive analyzer window would only
# add a way for the run to fail for a non-code reason.
